# G010 — Unit Converter

*v2. See `unit-converter.md` for prose: agents think in frames,
conversions happen at boundaries, ratio vs offset conversions differ
structurally, normalise-to-base as the resolver's generic pattern.*

---

## Choreographic Case: International Shipping Coordination

[international-shipping
    IN ORDER TO
        let one warehouse report in km and one carrier quote in miles
        translate at the boundary without either agent changing its frame
        and return a unified shipping cost in USD

    concurrent [
        @warehouse-tokyo{distance-to-port: "nearest"} -> @dist-km
    |
        @carrier-us{rate-schedule: "pacific-route"}   -> @rate-per-mile
    ]
    join

    @dist-mi <- [convert {value: @dist-km,
                           from: "km", to: "mi"}]
    @cost    <- [calc    {expr: "@dist-mi * @rate-per-mile"}]

    @warehouse-tokyo{confirm: "shipping cost is " + @cost + " USD"}
]

---

## Verb Surface

[convert
    value           {value, from, to}         ;; ratio or offset, dispatched
    to-base         {value, from}              ;; -> value in canonical unit
    from-base       {value, to}                ;; -> value in target unit
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: offset vs ratio dispatched by `where` unit kind

[convert {value: @v, from: @a, to: @b}]
    IN ORDER TO
        select the correct compose rule because ratio and offset
        conversions do NOT compose the same way

    @a and @b
        where both are ratio-units (mass, distance, volume, …)
            -> (@v * ratio-to-base[@a]) / ratio-to-base[@b]
        where both are offset-units (temperature)
            -> to-base(@v, @a)  ;; apply offset + scale
               then from-base(→, @b)  ;; apply inverse-scale + offset
        otherwise
            -> #incompatible-units
]

Ratio conversions chain: km→m→ft is equivalent to km→ft.
Offset conversions don't: you cannot chain C→F→K by multiplying
factors. The resolver must know which kind it's dispatching.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: frame translation at `where` boundaries meet

The warehouse thinks in kilometres.
The carrier thinks in miles.
Neither needs to know the other's frame.
The choreography translates at the boundary, once.

---

## Demo

[demo
    -> [convert {value: 100,  from: "celsius",    to: "fahrenheit"}]
    ;; -> 212.0

    -> [convert {value: 1,    from: "mi",         to: "km"}]
    ;; -> 1.609344

    -> [convert {value: 2.5,  from: "kg",         to: "lb"}]
    ;; -> 5.51156

    -> [convert {value: 1,    from: "gal",        to: "L"}]
    ;; -> 3.78541
]
